  "sections": [
    {
      "contentId": 17592186173158,
      "id": "3c0df1c3-781f-5384-8242-e29a5af8b45d",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "A function relates an input to an output.\nThe set of all inputs is called the **domain** of the function, while the set of all *possible* outputs is the **codomain** of the function.\n\nTo say \"*the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y,$*\" we can use the following notation:\n\n$$\nf : X \\to Y\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nYou might be used to calling the set of outputs of a function as the *range* of the function. However, there is a subtle difference between codomain and range, which we'll learn about during this lesson.",
      "mathAcademyId": "5041",
      "stepId": "17592186172893",
      "title": "Introduction"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186171837,
      "id": "1cfa3d7e-ad4a-56eb-a574-b1aabd5cdaac",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186172031,
        "title": "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"
      },
      "markdown": "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\nI. $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\nIII. $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-8474",
      "stepId": "17592186172894",
      "title": "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation",
      "workedSolution": "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186171838,
      "id": "d0715f6e-ea8f-5320-8fcf-cb71f9bc111a",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186172032,
        "title": "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"
      },
      "markdown": "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\nI. $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nII. $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\nIII. $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-8475",
      "stepId": "17592186172895",
      "title": "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation",
      "workedSolution": "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186173159,
      "id": "94c9cb06-fe8d-5820-b507-531956879003",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "The alternative arrow notation for a function \"$\\mapsto$\" describes to which element of the codomain each element in the domain is mapped.\n\nConsider the following function $f,$ defined by the alternative arrow notation:\n\n$$\nf : x \\mapsto |x|\n$$\n\nThis means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $|x|$ in the codomain. Notice that this can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = |x|.\n$$",
      "mathAcademyId": "5042",
      "stepId": "17592186172897",
      "title": "An Alternative Arrow Notation"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186171839,
      "id": "838c5337-7b11-5602-b176-1ada2e0f217d",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186172033,
        "title": "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"
      },
      "markdown": "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\nI. $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\nII. $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\nIII. $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-8476",
      "stepId": "17592186172896",
      "title": "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations",
      "workedSolution": "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186173160,
      "id": "cb125237-85d5-5a53-b57b-8dc014be36e0",
      "kind": "tutorial",
      "knowledgePoint": null,
      "markdown": "How do the codomain and range differ? Recall that in the notation $f : X \\to Y$ the codomain of the function is $Y.$\n\nOn the other hand, the range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y \\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x \\in X.$\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=|x|$ shown in the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/28/28fc79adea3f40c735cc0015069b4dc30433b7a3d372cc00148d6412a8ba27af.png)\n\nInspecting the diagram, we see that:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X = \\{ -2,-1,1,2,3 \\},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y = \\{ 0,1,2,3,4 \\},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is $\\{ 1,2,3 \\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nThe range depends on the domain we choose. If we remove the element $3$ from the domain in the diagram above, then the element $3$ in the codomain will not have any incoming arrows. Therefore, the range of the function will be $\\{ 1,2 \\}.$",
      "mathAcademyId": "5045",
      "stepId": "17592186172898",
      "title": "Codomain vs. Range"
    },
    {
      "contentId": 17592186171840,
      "id": "cc522bcd-087e-5658-be5c-e4da5fa77ba5",
      "kind": "example",
      "knowledgePoint": {
        "id": 17592186172034,
        "title": "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"
      },
      "markdown": "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$",
      "mathAcademyId": "e-8487",
      "stepId": "17592186172899",
      "title": "Identifying Codomains and Ranges",
      "workedSolution": "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""
    }
  ],
